The only way to get a `List` of the `List` elements is to use a *nested* `for`‑loop.  
The outer loop iterates over the outer `List`, the inner loop iterates over each
inner `List`.  Inside the inner loop you can add the element to a new
`List<List<Integer>>` (or any other collection) that you return.

```java
public static List<List<Integer>> flatten(List<List<Integer>> outer) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

    for (List<Integer> inner : outer) {          // outer loop
        for (Integer value : inner) {            // inner loop
            // create a new list for each element (or keep the whole inner list)
            List<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>();
            temp.add(value);
            result.add(temp);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

If you want to keep the inner lists intact, just add the `inner` list to
`result` inside the outer loop:

```java
for (List<Integer> inner : outer) {
    result.add(inner);   // keeps the original inner list
}
```
